We have enhanced our `t_operand` structure as follows:

typedef struct s_env    t_env;

typedef struct  s_op_token
{
	char        *cnt;
	t_quote_int quotes[MAX_QUOTES_NUM];

}   t_op_token;

typedef struct s_operand
{           
	char        *name; // Path to program with its arguments, redirections and etc.

	int         read_end;               // stdin
	int         write_end;              // stdout

	t_redir     redirs[MAX_REDIRS_NUM]; // Redirections of this operand-program
	size_t      red_cnt;                // Redirections counter

	t_quote_int quotes[MAX_QUOTES_NUM]; // All quotes pairs found in `name`
	size_t      qpair_cnt;              // Counter of quote pairs

	char        **argv;
	int         argc;

	t_op_token  *tokens;                // Tokens of this operand-program
	size_t      token_cnt;

	t_env       vars;					// Variables we created parsing this operand-program
	bool        f_per_cmd;              // Per-command flag for our variables

	pid_t       pid;

}   t_operand;

Each operand is gonna have its own array of tokens and environment variables that will be
copied into common array of environment variables.
We did preliminary redirections parsing where we just read heredocs and copied all other
redirections we found in `name`. We just copied all redirection paths as they are without
expanding anything. Later we'll be updating redirection paths if necessary. Also on this 
preliminary parsing stage we removed all syntax quotes in all heredoc delimiters and if
there were at least one quote pair in the delimiter we lowered `f_expand_body` flag (by
default is was set as true).

Now the updated algorithm.


1. Let's divide operand's string `name` on tokens by spaces
   (which are located outside any quote intervals of course).
   During initialization we store on heap in `cnt` token's content;

2. Update quote intervals for each token and and allocate memory for
   qmask[] filling it with IND_QNONE;

3. LOOP traversing all these tokens;

    3.1. IF token is an assignment (we found = outside any
	     quotes, on the left from = there are some symbols
		 and we haven't found any argument yet);
	    3.1.1. Check LHS (variable's name) for correctness;
		3.1.2. IF variable's name is not correct;
		    Return error;
		3.1.3. IF there is a ~ at the beginning located outside any quote intervals;
		    3.1.3.1. Expand ~;
			3.1.3.2. Update quote intervals for this token;
			3.1.3.3. LOOP through all token symbols;
			    3.1.3.3.1. IF we found $ outside single quotes;
			        3.1.3.3.1.1. Expand this variable (we don't use qmask[] here
								 because we're not gonna expand any wildcards);
				3.1.3.3.2. Remove all syntax quote pairs;
				3.1.3.3.3. Create local environment variable and add it into
						   the array of variables of this operand `vars`;
			3.1.3.4. END of token symbols LOOP;

	3.2. ELSE IF token is <, <<, > or >> ;
	    3.2.1. Just skip it;
	
	3.3. ELSE (Means current token is a regular argument or a redirection operand);
	    3.3.1. IF the previous token was << (means current token is a heredoc delimiter)
		    3.3.1.1. Just skip it (cause we have already processed it on preliminary parsing stage);
		3.3.2. IF there is a ~ at the beginning and next next symbol is / OR if ~ is the only
			   one in the token (needless to say all these simbols have to be located
			   outside any quotes);
		    3.3.2.1. Expand ~;
			3.3.2.2. Update quote intervals for this token;

			3.3.2.3. LOOP through all token symbols;
			    3.3.2.3.1. Mark all indices inside double quote intervals as IND_QDOUBLE in qmask[];
			    3.3.2.3.2. Mark all indices inside single quote intervals as IND_QSINGLE in qmask[];
			3.3.2.4. END of token symbols LOOP;

			3.3.2.5. LOOP through all token symbols;
			    3.3.2.5.1. IF a $ was found on the index which is marked as IND_QDOBLE
				           OR IND_QNONE in qmask[];
					Expand variable assigning all symbols the same quote state as $ had;
			3.3.2.6. END of token symbols LOOP;

			3.3.2.7. LOOP through all token symbols;
			    3.3.2.7.1. IF a * was found on the index which is marked as IND_QNONE in qmask[];
				(means the entire token is globbing mask)
				    3.3.2.7.1.1. Unite all consequtive * in one
					3.3.2.7.1.2. Expand this wildcard pattern passing the mask into expand_wildcards();
					3.3.2.8.1.3. Break;
			3.3.2.6. END of token symbols LOOP;

			3.3.2.7. IF the previous token was <, <<, or > (means we were handling redirection operand)
				3.3.2.7.1. Update the corresponding redirection's path
			3.3.2.8 ELSE (means we were handling an argument)
			    3.3.2.8.1. Add this processed token into array of arguments `argv`;
				3.3.2.8.2. Mark all variables created while parsing current operand as per-command
						   variables (basically by setting `f_per_cmd`); It implyes we'll not
						   be creating any more variables while parsing the current operand;

4. END of token LOOP;
